; workload-proxy — an SELinux domain for the per-workload tinyproxy (ADR 006 §4.4).
;
; WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL
;
; Fedora's policy has squid_t and squid_exec_t but no tinyproxy domain, and
; upstream tinyproxy ships no policy files, so there is nothing to inherit or
; adapt — an unconfined tinyproxy would run unconfined_service_t. That matters
; more here than anywhere else in this project: the proxy is the one component
; that terminates guest-controlled input. A compromised guest reaches its own
; proxy before it reaches anything else.
;
; The module is host-global and mandatory, like security/workload-vm.cil and for
; the same reason: nothing in it is per-workload, so N copies through the
; per-bundle [security].selinux_policy mechanism would race semodule to install
; identical content. The RPM's %post loads it.
;
; SIZE, AND WHY THAT NUMBER WAS THE POINT
;
; The design tracked "how large is the delta for a confined tinyproxy?" as its
; last open question, because the answer could reopen the tinyproxy-over-squid
; choice: squid's advantage is precisely that squid_t already exists. Measured
; on Fedora 44 / selinux-policy 44.5, the answer is 14 allow rules and no
; namespace machinery — smaller than the virtiofsd domain next door. The choice
; stands.
;
; HARVESTING THIS IS NOT LIKE HARVESTING wlvfsd_t
;
; A permissive run is close to useless for tinyproxy. It exits 70 the moment it
; cannot read its own config, so a permissive harvest records the first gate and
; nothing behind it — the run "succeeds" at logging and tells you almost
; nothing. Four enforcing iterations produced this set, each revealing exactly
; one further gate. Re-derive it that way, not from a single permissive pass.
;
; /usr/bin, NOT /usr/sbin. Fedora's /usr/sbin is a symlink to bin, so the RPM's
; /usr/sbin/tinyproxy resolves to /usr/bin/tinyproxy — and a filecon naming the
; symlinked path matches nothing at all, silently. (matchpathcon reports bin_t
; while the entry sits visibly in file_contexts, which is a confusing 20
; minutes if you have not seen it before.)

(type wlproxy_t)
(typeattributeset domain (wlproxy_t))
(roletype system_r wlproxy_t)

; roletype before the filecon, or semodule rejects the module with
; "Type wlproxy_exec_t is invalid for role object_r".
(type wlproxy_exec_t)
(roletype object_r wlproxy_exec_t)
(typeattributeset file_type (wlproxy_exec_t))
(typeattributeset exec_type (wlproxy_exec_t))
(typeattributeset entry_type (wlproxy_exec_t))
(filecon "/usr/bin/tinyproxy" file (system_u object_r wlproxy_exec_t ((s0) (s0))))

; Retyping the binary is what keeps the entrypoint narrow. Left as bin_t the
; domain would need `bin_t:file entrypoint`, which every other bin_t binary
; would then also be able to enter this domain through.
;
; Unlike QEMU (which needs a runcon wrapper because there is no
; init_t -> svirt_t transition), the proxy transitions straight out of systemd:
; this module declares the transition itself, so the unit's ExecStart is the
; plain binary.
(typetransition init_t wlproxy_exec_t process wlproxy_t)
(allow init_t wlproxy_exec_t (file (getattr open read execute map)))
(allow init_t wlproxy_t (process (transition siginh)))
(allow wlproxy_t wlproxy_exec_t (file (entrypoint execute map getattr ioctl open read)))

; stdout/stderr to the journal.
(allow wlproxy_t init_t (unix_stream_socket (read write)))

; Sockets. Listener plus one connection per client, and a netlink_route socket
; that glibc opens to enumerate interfaces during resolution.
(allow wlproxy_t self (tcp_socket (accept bind connect create getattr getopt ioctl listen read setopt shutdown write)))
(allow wlproxy_t self (udp_socket (bind connect create getattr getopt ioctl read setopt write)))
(allow wlproxy_t self (netlink_route_socket (bind create getattr nlmsg_read read write)))

; The listener. squid_port_t is not a typo: 3128 is squid's registered port and
; the policy labels it accordingly, so a confined tinyproxy on the conventional
; proxy port needs a name_bind on squid's label. node_bind covers binding to the
; workload's own management address rather than a wildcard.
(allow wlproxy_t squid_port_t (tcp_socket (name_bind)))
(allow wlproxy_t node_t (tcp_socket (node_bind)))

; Outbound. http_port_t covers 80 and 443 both — this is the whole of what the
; proxy may reach by port, and the hostname allowlist is what narrows it
; further. Note there is no name_connect for the DNS lookups: SELinux checks
; name_connect on TCP only, so the UDP query to the host resolver is covered by
; the self:udp_socket rule above. DNS is not filterable here.
(allow wlproxy_t http_port_t (tcp_socket (name_connect)))

; Resolver configuration: /etc/resolv.conf (net_conf_t), which on a
; systemd-resolved host is a symlink into /run/systemd/resolve (init_var_run_t).
(allow wlproxy_t net_conf_t (file (getattr open read)))
(allow wlproxy_t net_conf_t (lnk_file (read)))
(allow wlproxy_t init_var_run_t (dir (search)))

; Its own runtime directory: /run/workload-vm/<name>, which workload-vm.cil's
; sibling arrangement labels qemu_var_run_t. Config and hostname allowlist are
; read; log and pid file are created, written and removed. This is the whole of
; the proxy's filesystem surface — it has no access to the workload's disks,
; volumes, or state directory, which is the point of it being a separate domain
; from svirt_t rather than sharing one.
(allow wlproxy_t qemu_var_run_t (dir (add_name getattr open read remove_name search write)))
(allow wlproxy_t qemu_var_run_t (file (append create getattr lock open read setattr unlink write)))
